Simon Richard Clarkstone <s.r.clarkst...@durham.ac.uk> wrote:

> This has reminded me of YANI for a non-aligned
> artefact: The Boots of Rincewind.

> Readers of the Discworld series will know the
> Rincewind is a below-level-0 wizard who is a
> professional coward, and thus excels at running
> away.  His boots would give you extreme speed
> (faster than normal boots of speed), but only when
> you were running away from danger.

> OST this is really YANSNI, as it would be a PITA
> to implement (unless you can nick the "fleeing"
> code) and they would not be too useful outside a
> small range of situations.  (Except for
> pacifists.)  And they might be good for weaponless
> monks who are sick of getting weapons from their
> gods.  We need more non-weapon artefacts!

This is an _excellent_ idea.

Make the boots "normal boots of speed", _except_
when moving in a direction with

1) no enemies in the quadrant toward which you are
moving, and

2) enemies in the quadrant away from which you are
moving.

This limits their use to "run away" situations. when
something like "double extreme speed" is allowed,
but nutrition consumption quadruples, and duration
without rest is limited by constitution.

The Boots of Rincewind would have to be a)pplied, so
you don't burn calories like this all the time, and
then a)pplied again to leave "run away faster" mode.

Jinking toward an enemy, or an enemy appearing in
front of the PC, would drop the PC out of "run away
faster" mode immediately, with an appropriate
message, maybe: "you slow to reconsider".

Should the mode be "run as far as possible in the
chosen direction" as if you'd given a shifted motion
command? That matches the description of what
Rincewind does, showing only heels.

FWIW

xanthian.

Fuel consumption for a ship at sea goes up as the
fourth power of velocity, so merely quadrupling food
use is an gross under-estimate, it should be 16-fold.




